      "dom_id": "question-82238",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation, which we do using cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3r & =h \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r=\\frac{h}{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ V & =\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\ V & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{{h}^{2}}{9}\\cdot h \\\\ V & =\\frac{\\pi {h}^{3}}{9} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =\\frac{9V}{\\pi } \\\\ h & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{9V}{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
